from curriculum_library import dossier

dossier(51,'阅读：公告与条件','A membership notice',
'Joining the center~New members may apply online or at reception. Membership begins when payment and identity checks are complete, not when the application is first saved. Applicants receive a confirmation showing the start date.||Using facilities~Ordinary membership includes the exercise room and pool during public sessions. Specialist classes require separate booking. A membership card does not guarantee a place in a class that is already full.||Suspending membership~Members unable to attend for a continuous month may request a temporary suspension before that month begins. Retrospective requests are considered only in exceptional circumstances supported by an explanation.||Ending membership~Monthly members must give notice by the date stated in their agreement. Stopping a bank payment without notifying the center may leave the membership active and fees outstanding.',
'A member\'s misunderstanding~I assumed that paying for membership allowed me to join any session. When a popular class was full, I complained at reception. Staff showed me the booking condition in my confirmation.||A useful distinction~Access to facilities and a reserved class place were different benefits. The wording was accurate, but the information had appeared far below the price on the website. I suggested making it more visible.||An immediate solution~The receptionist added me to the waiting list and explained the cancellation alerts. I did not receive a place that evening, but obtained one the following week.||A reading strategy~When comparing memberships, I now check exclusions, notice periods and booking requirements. Words such as includes, subject to and before often identify conditions more important than promotional descriptions.',
'A membership call~Caller: Can I suspend my membership for next month? Adviser: Yes, if you submit the request before the first.||Checking the period~Caller: I will be away for only two weeks. Adviser: The standard suspension covers a continuous month. We do not normally offer half-month suspensions.||A payment question~Caller: Should I cancel my bank payment? Adviser: No. Wait for our confirmation and follow the instructions there.||Confirming the record~Adviser: I will email the policy. Your membership remains active until an approved suspension is recorded.',
'A coworking pass~A ten-visit pass expires ninety days after purchase. Unused visits do not transfer automatically to a new pass.||Access hours~Pass holders may enter between eight and six on weekdays. Evening events require separate tickets, even when held in the same building.||Reservations~A visit may be reserved up to seven days ahead. Cancel before eight on the booked day to keep the visit credit.||Guests~A pass is personal. Bringing another person requires a guest booking; two people cannot share one visit credit.')

dossier(52,'阅读：匹配信息','Choosing a suitable service',
'The quick counter~The town repair counter handles minor clothing alterations while customers wait. It does not accept leather or items needing specialist cleaning. This option suits simple jobs with a short deadline.||The specialist workshop~A workshop outside town repairs leather bags and replaces unusual fittings. Estimates are free, but parts may take several weeks. Customers prioritizing specialist work over speed may prefer it.||The collection service~A mobile service collects curtains from local homes and returns them after alteration. Collection is included only within the stated district. Customers elsewhere pay an additional transport charge.||The teaching studio~A sewing studio offers supervised repair sessions for people who want to learn. Participants bring their own item and do the work themselves. It is not a drop-off alteration service.',
'Start with the requirement~A matching task may describe someone who needs collection, a particular material or a fixed deadline. Identify those requirements before comparing attractive but irrelevant features.||Keep all conditions~A service that handles the correct material may still fail the deadline. Matching only one keyword can lead to a plausible but unsuitable answer. Each essential condition must be satisfied.||Notice exclusions~Phrases such as except, only within and subject to availability can change the decision. They often appear after the main description, so reading only the first sentence is risky.||Use evidence~For each choice, identify the words supporting it and the condition ruling out the closest alternative. This turns the task into a comparison of evidence rather than a guess based on familiar vocabulary.',
'A customer request~Customer: I need a leather bag repaired before Friday. Counter assistant: We do not handle leather here. The specialist workshop does, but you must ask about timing.||An alternative~Customer: Can your collection service take it? Assistant: That service is for curtains only. Collection does not imply every item is accepted.||Checking urgency~Customer: Could the workshop promise Friday? Assistant: I cannot speak for them. If a special fitting is needed, the repair may take longer.||Next action~Assistant: Take a photograph and contact the workshop directly. Tell them both the material and your deadline.',
'Service A~A language club offers informal conversation on weekday mornings. It has no examinations and welcomes all adult levels.||Service B~A tutor provides evening interview practice by appointment. Sessions focus on job applications rather than general conversation.||Service C~A college offers a fixed twelve-week examination course with assessed homework. Attendance is required on two evenings each week.||Service D~An online library provides recordings and transcripts for independent study. It offers no live conversation or individual feedback.')

dossier(53,'阅读：事实与缺失信息','What the text does not say',
'A stated fact~A sports club opened a new training room in March. The notice says existing members can use it without an additional fee. It does not state whether the club\'s ordinary membership price changed that year.||A contradiction~The notice requires advance booking for coached sessions. A claim that members may attend coached sessions without booking contradicts this rule. The relevant issue is booking, not whether entry is free.||Missing information~The notice gives no comparison with other clubs. A claim that this is the cheapest training room in the town cannot be confirmed or contradicted from the notice alone.||Avoiding outside knowledge~A reader may know another local club\'s prices, but that knowledge does not supply missing evidence within a reading task. The answer must follow the passage and the question instructions.',
'A research summary~Twenty volunteers tested a travel-planning website for one week. Eighteen completed the assigned booking task. The report does not say whether they had used similar websites before.||An unjustified inference~It would be unsafe to conclude that all beginners found the website easy. The participants\' experience is unknown, and completing a task does not reveal how much effort or help it required.||A direct conflict~If a statement says only ten volunteers completed the task, it conflicts with the reported number. No additional interpretation is needed to identify the disagreement.||A careful response~Separate what is explicitly stated, what follows logically and what remains unknown. A believable statement is not automatically supported, and missing information is not the same as a false statement.',
'Discussing a report~Student: The passage says twelve people joined the trial. Does that mean twelve completed it? Tutor: No. Look for a separate completion figure.||Finding the evidence~Student: It says nine submitted the final questionnaire. Tutor: That tells us about questionnaire submission, not necessarily every part of the trial.||Avoiding a guess~Student: They probably completed everything. Tutor: Probably is not enough. The text may leave that question unanswered.||A distinction~Tutor: A contradiction requires evidence against the claim. Silence about the claim is a different situation.',
'A museum trial~The museum offered free entry on three Wednesdays in May. Visitor numbers on those days were higher than on the previous Wednesdays.||A stated limitation~The report notes that school holidays overlapped with one free-entry day. It does not estimate how much this affected attendance.||An unknown outcome~No information is provided about money spent in the museum shop. Higher attendance alone does not establish higher shop income.||A future decision~The museum will review the trial before deciding whether to repeat it. The report does not announce permanent free Wednesday admission.')

dossier(54,'阅读：段落主旨','Finding the main point',
'A broad problem~A delivery company faced rising complaints, including late arrivals, damaged packages and unclear updates. Managers initially looked for a single cause, but the complaints described several different failures.||A targeted investigation~Staff grouped complaints by type and delivery area. Delays were concentrated on two routes, while damage appeared across the network. This suggested that different problems required different responses.||A communication improvement~The company introduced clearer tracking messages. Customers could see when a delay had been confirmed rather than repeatedly receiving the original expected time. Better information did not itself make the vehicle arrive sooner.||A remaining limitation~Complaints about uncertainty fell, but damaged packages remained a concern. The company then reviewed packing and handling. Improvement in one measure should not be mistaken for resolution of the entire problem.',
'A heading is a summary~A useful heading captures the paragraph\'s main function. It may describe a problem, a proposed solution, a qualification or a result. One memorable example may support the point without being the point itself.||Read the whole paragraph~The opening sentence often introduces a topic, but a later sentence may change or qualify it. Stopping after the first familiar keyword can hide the paragraph\'s actual conclusion.||Compare close alternatives~Two headings may share vocabulary while expressing different relationships. A reason for a change is not the same as evidence that the change succeeded. Ask what the paragraph actually does.||Check the fit~After selecting a heading, explain how the remaining sentences support it. If most sentences do not fit, reconsider whether the heading captures only a minor detail.',
'A learner\'s choice~Learner: I chose "Faster deliveries" because the paragraph mentions late parcels. Tutor: Does it say deliveries became faster?||Looking again~Learner: It mainly describes clearer messages to customers. Tutor: Then the heading should reflect communication, not an outcome the paragraph does not claim.||A competing option~Learner: "Reducing uncertainty" fits better. Tutor: Yes, because customers learned what was happening even when a delay remained.||Transfer~Tutor: Apply the same check to the next paragraph. Identify the main claim before comparing the available headings.',
'An initial assumption~A college believed low library attendance reflected declining interest in books. It had not yet asked students why they stayed away.||New evidence~Interviews revealed that opening hours conflicted with classes and part-time jobs. Several students used other libraries instead.||A revised service~The college tested two late evenings and made quiet study rooms available during that period. Book borrowing rules did not change.||An unresolved issue~Attendance rose, but computer availability remained limited. Longer hours addressed one barrier without eliminating all difficulties.')

dossier(55,'听力：数字与改口','Listen for the final arrangement',
'Why first answers mislead~In a booking conversation, a speaker may suggest one time and then revise it. Writing the first number heard can therefore produce an error even when every word was recognized correctly.||Language signalling change~Expressions such as actually, instead and make that often introduce a correction. The listener should connect the final value with the right item rather than replacing every number indiscriminately.||Keeping categories separate~A date, room number and price may occur close together. Brief notes with labels help distinguish them. An accurate number in the wrong field is still an incorrect answer.||Checking task limits~A form may allow only a number or a small number of words. Understanding the conversation is essential, but the final response must also follow the written instruction.',
'A booking record~The initial enquiry requested Room Twelve on 14 June. The organizer then discovered that the group would be larger than expected and asked about a room with more seats.||A revised room~The center offered Room Twenty, which held thirty people. The date remained 14 June. Changing the room did not change every other part of the booking.||A price correction~The first quoted fee included a projector. Since the organizer did not need one, the final fee was reduced. The written confirmation separated the room charge from optional equipment.||A final check~The organizer read back the date, room and total before paying. This short check caught a mistaken digit in the contact number, preventing a later communication problem.',
'The request~Caller: I would like six places for the workshop on the seventeenth. Receptionist: The seventeenth is full, but the nineteenth has space.||The correction~Caller: The nineteenth works. Actually, make that five places; one colleague cannot attend. Receptionist: Five on the nineteenth.||The cost~Receptionist: Each place is twelve pounds, so the total is sixty. Caller: Does that include materials? Receptionist: Yes, but not lunch.||The final detail~Caller: Please send confirmation to my work email. My contact number ends in 603, not 630.',
'An equipment booking~A community group originally requested twelve chairs and two tables. After receiving more registrations, it increased the chair order to eighteen.||An unchanged detail~The number of tables remained two. The group did not require an additional table because refreshments would be served from a built-in counter.||A revised collection~Collection moved from Thursday afternoon to Friday morning because the equipment store was closed for inspection.||A payment condition~The deposit was refundable after all items were returned undamaged. It was not a discount on the hire charge.')

dossier(56,'听力：地图与方位','Following a tour',
'Choose a reference point~Directions are easier to follow when the listener knows the starting point and facing direction. Left and right can change when a person turns; north and south do not change in the same way.||Track the movement~A guide may describe a route through several landmarks. Note the sequence rather than treating each direction as an isolated fact. Passing a building is different from entering it.||Notice changes~Temporary closures can make a familiar route unavailable. A tour recording may mention the old entrance before describing the current one. The listener must identify which arrangement now applies.||Avoid visual guessing~A location that looks convenient on a diagram may still be wrong. Use the spoken relationship between landmarks and check it against the map rather than assuming the nearest symbol is the answer.',
'A visitor route~Begin at the information desk facing the courtyard. The exhibition hall is on your left. The cafe is beyond the courtyard, beside the glass doors leading to the garden.||An access alternative~The steps beside the hall are the shortest route to the upper gallery. Visitors needing level access should use the lift behind the desk. Both routes reach the same gallery.||A temporary closure~The west entrance is closed during repairs. Deliveries use the service gate, while visitors should continue using the main entrance. The service gate is not an alternative public entrance.||Leaving the building~At the end of the visit, follow the marked exit through the shop. Visitors are not required to make a purchase. Emergency exits remain available but are not the ordinary departure route.',
'Starting the tour~Guide: We are facing the main courtyard. The workshop is the low building to your right, and the cafe is directly ahead.||A changed location~Guide: Bicycle parking used to be beside the cafe. It is now behind the workshop, away from the delivery route.||Finding the gallery~Visitor: Where is the upper gallery? Guide: Use the stairs on the left or the lift immediately behind us.||A restriction~Guide: The gate beyond the garden is for staff only. At the end of the tour, please return through the main building.',
'A campus entrance~Visitors enter the college through the south gate and follow the path to reception. The sports building is passed on the left.||Finding the hall~From reception, turn right at the first corridor. The lecture hall is the second door, opposite the student kitchen.||An accessible route~A ramp beside the south gate provides step-free access. The north gate has steps and is not the recommended route for wheelchair users.||Parking~Visitor parking is outside the east boundary. A parking permit does not allow vehicles onto the central pedestrian paths.')

dossier(57,'听力：多人观点','Who agrees with what?',
'Different speaker roles~In a group discussion, one person may describe a proposal while another evaluates it. Reporting an idea does not necessarily mean supporting it. The listener needs to connect each opinion to the correct speaker.||Qualified agreement~A speaker may agree with the aim but reject the method. Words such as provided, unless and only if restrict the agreement. A simple agree-or-disagree note may lose the important condition.||Changing a position~New evidence can persuade someone to revise an initial view. The final position may differ from the opening statement. Listening only for the first positive or negative phrase is unreliable.||Using notes carefully~Short notes identifying the speaker, view and reason are often more useful than a full transcript. The aim is to preserve relationships between ideas, not to write every word.',
'A project discussion~Mira proposed surveying all customers, believing a large sample would be more convincing. Dan worried that the team lacked time to analyze hundreds of open responses.||A different concern~Elena pointed out that surveying only current customers would miss those who had stopped using the service. She preferred a smaller sample covering both groups.||A revised proposal~Mira accepted that sample variety mattered more than size alone. The group chose a short questionnaire for two groups, followed by a few detailed interviews.||An unresolved issue~Dan still wanted to test the questions before sending them widely. Agreement on the sample did not mean every part of the research design had been settled.',
'Initial views~Mira: We should ask every customer. Dan: I like the ambition, but we cannot analyze that much writing by Friday.||Another perspective~Elena: I am more concerned about missing former customers. They may explain why people leave.||A change~Mira: That is a good point. Let us use a smaller mixed sample rather than only current customers.||A condition~Dan: I can support that if we test the questions first. A small sample will not help if the questions are unclear.',
'A planning group~Arun favors an outdoor event because the park offers more space. Bea supports the larger venue but worries about rain.||An alternative~Chen suggests hiring a tent. Bea agrees only if the hire cost stays within the existing budget.||A revised view~After seeing the tent quotation, Arun proposes a smaller indoor event instead. His final preference differs from his opening suggestion.||A remaining task~The group has not yet booked either venue. Chen will check indoor availability before a decision is confirmed.')

dossier(58,'写作：任务回应','Answering the actual question',
'A familiar topic trap~A learner sees a question about working from home and writes everything remembered about technology. The essay may contain relevant vocabulary while failing to address the specific comparison or judgement requested.||Identify the instruction~Discuss both views, give reasons and propose solutions require different responses. The topic tells the writer what to discuss; the instruction tells the writer what to do with it.||Develop rather than list~A paragraph with four undeveloped reasons can be weaker than one clear reason explained with consequences and an example. More ideas do not automatically produce a stronger argument.||Review against the prompt~Before submitting, compare each part of the question with the answer. A conclusion cannot repair an essay that has ignored half the task throughout the main paragraphs.',
'An example task~Some people think employers should allow all staff to work from home, while others believe the workplace is essential. Discuss both views and give your own opinion.||A balanced outline~One paragraph can explain flexibility and reduced commuting. Another can discuss collaboration, equipment and roles requiring physical presence. The writer then needs a clear position accounting for those differences.||Avoid an absolute claim~Saying every job can be done remotely is difficult to support. A more precise argument distinguishes tasks and circumstances rather than relying on a universal statement.||A coherent conclusion~A conclusion supporting flexible arrangements should follow from the body. It should not suddenly claim that offices should disappear if the discussion has emphasized their continuing value.',
'Reviewing an outline~Tutor: What does the question ask you to do? Learner: Discuss both views and give my opinion.||Finding a gap~Tutor: Your outline has three reasons for remote work. Where is the other view? Learner: I have not included it yet.||Developing balance~Tutor: Add a reason why some tasks need a shared workplace, then explain whether that changes your position.||A final check~Tutor: Balance does not require pretending both views are equally convincing. It requires responding fairly to the instruction before explaining your judgement.',
'A different essay task~A question asks why some adults stop learning new skills and what could encourage them to continue. It requires causes and possible responses.||An incomplete answer~An essay describing only the benefits of education does not directly explain either why people stop or how participation could improve.||A linked response~A barrier such as unpredictable working hours can be paired with flexible scheduling. A proposed solution should address an identified cause rather than appear as an unrelated suggestion.||A realistic qualification~Flexible scheduling will not solve every barrier. Cost, confidence and access may require different forms of support.')

dossier(59,'口语：延伸与追问','Beyond a memorized answer',
'A prepared opening~A learner can describe a favorite place fluently after practising the same answer repeatedly. However, unexpected questions reveal difficulty explaining reasons or comparing it with other places.||Useful development~An answer can expand through a reason, a concrete example and a consequence. This gives the listener something meaningful to follow without requiring a complicated phrase in every sentence.||Repairing a breakdown~When a word is missing, a simple description may keep the conversation moving. Asking for clarification can also be appropriate when a question is unclear. Silence while searching for one perfect word is not the only option.||Transfer practice~Changing the place, audience or follow-up question tests flexibility. Recording both attempts helps distinguish a memorized performance from improved spontaneous expression.',
'A personal example~I learned to cook a simple meal when I moved away from home. At first I followed instructions exactly because I did not understand which ingredients could be changed.||An obstacle~The first meal took much longer than expected. I had not prepared the vegetables before heating the pan. This taught me to read the whole recipe and organize the ingredients first.||A wider reflection~Learning the skill made daily life cheaper and more independent. It also helped me understand that practical knowledge develops through mistakes as well as explanations.||A possible follow-up~If asked whether everyone should learn to cook, I would discuss basic independence while recognizing that time, disability and access to equipment differ. A personal story can lead to a broader discussion without proving a universal rule.',
'An interview practice~Coach: Tell me about a useful skill you learned. Learner: I learned basic cooking after moving away from home.||A follow-up~Coach: What was difficult at first? Learner: I understood the recipe but did not organize the steps well. Preparing ingredients in advance helped.||A wider question~Coach: Can videos replace practical lessons? Learner: They can demonstrate steps, but a beginner may still need feedback when something goes wrong.||A challenge~Coach: Would your answer change for a dangerous activity? Learner: Yes. Safety risks may make qualified supervision much more important.',
'A speaking club~Members receive an unfamiliar everyday topic and one minute to prepare. Notes may contain keywords but not a complete script.||Interaction~After a short response, another member asks two follow-up questions. The speaker is encouraged to explain and clarify rather than repeat the original answer unchanged.||Feedback~Listeners identify one clear idea and one place where understanding became difficult. They do not assign an official examination score.||A second attempt~The speaker answers a related but different question. The aim is to apply feedback while avoiding a word-for-word repetition.')

dossier(60,'阶段检查：分科计时','A realistic practice week',
'Separate purposes~A practice week can include a full reading section one day and writing another day. This fits a sixty-minute daily limit, but it does not reproduce the fatigue of completing several examination sections consecutively.||Appropriate conditions~For a useful comparison, the learner records the source, time limit, interruptions and any assistance. A score obtained with a dictionary should not be presented as independent timed performance.||Feedback quality~Listening and reading answers can be checked against an appropriate key. Writing and speaking require judgement across several criteria. An automated estimate has uncertainty and should remain labelled accordingly.||Deciding what follows~The learner uses the weakest evidence to choose the next practice focus. Repeating the same test until the score rises may show familiarity rather than a comparable improvement in general ability.',
'A practice schedule~Monday is reserved for an unfamiliar reading section, with review on Tuesday. Wednesday contains listening practice and analysis. Thursday covers speaking, Friday timed writing, and Saturday writing revision.||Protecting the budget~On a sixty-minute reading or writing day, ordinary vocabulary and grammar tasks are replaced rather than added. Otherwise, a nominal one-hour plan would quietly become much longer.||Recording interpretation~The weekly summary distinguishes independent first attempts from revised work. Both are useful: the first shows current performance, while revision shows how feedback was applied.||An honest conclusion~Even strong results across separate days do not establish readiness for the full examination schedule. The summary describes a four-skill diagnostic and notes the untested endurance requirement.',
'Planning with a learner~Coach: Which skill needs the most reliable evidence this week? Learner: Reading. My previous score came from a passage I had already seen.||Selecting material~Coach: Use an unfamiliar official practice section and its instructions. Keep the answer key closed until time ends.||Handling interruptions~Learner: What if a call interrupts me? Coach: Record the interruption. You can finish for practice, but label the result so it is not compared as an uninterrupted attempt.||Next day~Coach: Review why each wrong answer was attractive and where the text supports the correct one. Do not just copy the answer key.',
'A workplace assessment~A training provider allows participants to complete four skill checks on separate days. Each result records the date and conditions.||Scoring~Objective items use a published answer key. Written responses receive comments from a reviewer, with any estimated level clearly identified as an estimate.||Retakes~A retake uses a different task where available. If the same task is repeated, the record shows that it was previously seen.||Certification~Completing the checks produces a course progress report, not an external language-test certificate.')
